Four probes done. SOAP is blocked at NetSuite auth config, not by the API's own validator — an admin toggle away from being the answer. Assemblies WORK via REST — first-class, no group-like block. Both findings shape 8c-3 AND the v1.1/v2 roadmap. Details:

1 · SOAP · Auth blocked at integration-record level, not by SOAP itself

Built SOAP TBA request with the canonical HMAC-SHA256 tokenPassport signature over account&consumerKey&tokenId&nonce&timestamp with key consumerSecret&tokenSecret. Tested against SuiteTalk WSDL 2024_2 (also 2023_2, 2022_2, 2020_2 for good measure).

Results:

┌─────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│           SOAP op           │      Auth required?      │                                Result                                 │
├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ getDataCenterUrls           │ no (discovery)           │ 200 webservicesDomain: https://7924416-sb2.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com │
├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ getServerTime               │ yes (minimal auth check) │ 500 Invalid login attempt. USER_ERROR                                 │
├─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ add SalesOrder + Group line │ yes                      │ 500 same Invalid login attempt.                                       │
└─────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Same TBA credentials work fine on REST simultaneously (REST GET returns 400 for a missing arg, which means "auth passed, argument validation failed" — auth is intact). The failure is specifically at SOAP-side auth on any auth-required op.

Cause: NetSuite integration records have separate TBA_AUTHORIZATION_FLOW, REST_WEB_SERVICES, and SOAP_WEB_SERVICES toggles. The Nexus integration record has TBA + REST enabled; SOAP is likely NOT enabled on the record itself, even if SOAP is enabled at the account-feature level.

To unblock SOAP: NetSuite admin (Melinda or whoever manages the integration record) toggles SOAP_WEB_SERVICES on the Nexus TBA integration in Setup → Integration → Manage Integrations → [Nexus]. ~1 minute of admin work. Once enabled, my SOAP add + probe re-runs immediately. If SOAP add then accepts the group line, we've found the answer and the swap in sales-orders.ts is ~50 LOC of SOAP-envelope authoring in place of the REST call.

If SOAP add STILL refuses group lines after enable, that's a separate probe outcome we can address then. But we don't know yet — the auth block means Probe 1 is inconclusive, not "SOAP failed."

2 · Assemblies WORK via REST — sandbox POST + delete confirmed

DPS has 9 Assembly items in the sandbox catalog today: Berry Hanks Hydration Assembly, BrainMD 355 Hydration 20ct Pouch(Assembly)), BrainMD-351 Brain Boost-Assembly, Fanny Bum Butter Assembly, Joop Skin Assembly, others. Not zero — DPS already uses them.

Test POST via REST:
POST /salesOrder
{
  entity: {id: "131860"}, subsidiary: {id: "2"}, orderStatus: "B",
  memo: "PROBE 4 — Assembly line",
  item: {items: [{item: {id: "69378"}, quantity: 10, rate: 5.0}]}  // Berry Hanks Hydration Assembly
}
→ 204 CREATED  soId=359541
transactionline:
  seq=0                                                         amount=50
  seq=1 item=69378 rate=11.023113 qty=-4.5359237 amount=-50    (UoM conversion — NS auto-scaled)
  seq=2 item=-8   (tax code line)
DELETE cleanup: ✓

REST accepts Assembly lines cleanly. No "Please enter a value for Amount" error. NetSuite handles per-line amount internally (Assemblies carry a build+price on the item record, not derived-from-members at SO-create time). The UoM conversion visible above (10 units × 5.0 = 50, then internally scaled to lb-based quantity — that's NS's item UoM logic, expected).

This is the strategic finding CA framed. REST is not "hostile to bundled items". It's "hostile to Item Groups specifically". Item Groups are a presentation wrapper with no build model; Assemblies are first-class NS records with a build process, component consumption, inventory transformation. REST models the latter; refuses to bear the responsibility of the former.

Nexus's ASY/LEAF model IS an assembly-shaped model — parent SKU + children + qty_per_parent + roll-up pricing. Not a group-shaped model. The v1.1/v2 case for a NetSuite-Assembly migration gets materially stronger with this data.

Assembly migration is out of Slice 12 scope but bank as a top v1.1 candidate.

3 · REST documentation search — no citable stated limitation

Public NetSuite docs at docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/ require authentication and my guessed URL paths returned 404. Metadata catalog endpoint requires an NS admin account distinct from Nexus's TBA integration.

Given the empirical certainty (REST refuses every attempted group POST shape; SOAP internally uses a different code path via the SuiteScript record engine which is why the UI works), the limitation is real but I can't cite a URL. Best doc source in the account would be: NetSuite admin logs into Documentation > REST Web Services in-app and searches for "item group" — that's the closest authoritative answer. Same person who'd enable SOAP could do this.

Ready to escalate the "search NS docs" work to Melinda (5 min in the NS UI).

4 · RESTlet feasibility — assessment only

What a RESTlet would contain, roughly:

// scripts/netsuite-suitescript/nexus-so-create.js
define(['N/record'], function(record) {
  function post(context) {
    // context is our JSON — {customer, subsidiary, memo, groupItemId, memberRates: [...]}
    var so = record.create({ type: 'salesorder' });
    so.setValue({fieldId: 'entity',      value: context.customer});
    so.setValue({fieldId: 'subsidiary',  value: context.subsidiary});
    so.setValue({fieldId: 'orderstatus', value: context.orderStatus});
    so.setValue({fieldId: 'memo',        value: context.memo});
    // custbody fields...
    // Add the group line — SuiteScript's native record engine handles
    // group expansion the same way the UI does
    so.selectNewLine({sublistId: 'item'});
    so.setCurrentSublistValue({sublistId: 'item', fieldId: 'item', value: context.groupItemId});
    so.setCurrentSublistValue({sublistId: 'item', fieldId: 'quantity', value: context.quantity});
    so.commitLine({sublistId: 'item'});
    // Iterate expanded members + set rates
    var count = so.getLineCount({sublistId: 'item'});
    for (var i = 0; i < count; i++) {
      var lineItem = so.getSublistValue({sublistId: 'item', line: i, fieldId: 'item'});
      var memberRate = context.memberRates[lineItem];
      if (memberRate !== undefined) {
        so.setSublistValue({sublistId: 'item', line: i, fieldId: 'rate', value: memberRate});
      }
    }
    var soId = so.save();
    return {soId: soId};
  }
  return {post: post};
});

Size: ~60 LOC of SuiteScript. Deploy via NS UI (Customization → Scripting → New RESTlet). Nexus calls it via a single REST POST to /services/rest/scripts/{scriptId}/{deploymentId} with the payload above.

Ownership: NetSuite-side code lives in the NS account. Anyone with Customization role can view/edit. Standing risk — DPS has a history of undocumented automation nobody remaining can explain (per CA). Adding another script to that pile is real cost. Mitigations: script name explicitly nexus_so_create, README + inline docstring naming Slice 12 + link back to this repo, versioned via NS's built-in script versioning.

Estimate:
- SuiteScript authoring: 4-6 hours (well-known pattern; the tricky bit is verifying the group-expansion + member-rate iteration behaves as the UI does)
- Nexus-side integration change: ~30 LOC (replace createSalesOrder's REST call with RESTlet invocation; same idempotency-key semantics via a custom header the SuiteScript reads)
- Deployment: ~1 hour (upload to NS, deploy, test in sandbox, get script/deployment IDs)
- Testing: 4 hours (sandbox smoke covering the 3 failure paths, plus at least one live-fixture end-to-end)

Total ballpark: 1-1.5 days for CC + 1 day for CA/Edward review + a Melinda-side ownership handoff conversation.

Recommendation

Order of operations, cheapest-first:

1. Ask Melinda (or NS admin) to toggle SOAP_WEB_SERVICES on the Nexus integration record. ~1 minute. Then re-run Probe 1's add SalesOrder — if SOAP accepts the group line, we have our answer with a REST-vs-SOAP swap in sales-orders.ts (~50 LOC).
2. In parallel, ask the same person to search NS Help for a stated REST + Item Group limitation. If documented, use as citable reason for the pivot. If not documented, escalate as a NetSuite support ticket (Melinda-side; not blocking Slice 12).
3. If SOAP works — swap the call, finish smoke, ship 8c-3 with full grouped-SO capability. Ideal.
4. If SOAP is also refused — RESTlet is a real cost but a real answer. Alternative: (a) flat lines now + Assembly migration on the roadmap. My lean at that decision point would be flat lines now, Assembly migration is the strategic answer, RESTlet becomes obsolete once Assemblies land. But we're one Melinda-toggle away from not having to make that decision.

